Q: Javascript RegExp + Word boundaries + unicode characters A: <p>There appears to be a problem with Regex and the word boundary <code>\b</code> matching the beginning of a string with a starting character out of the normal 256 byte range.</p>
<p>Instead of using <code>\b</code>, try using <code>(?:^|$|\\s)</code></p>
<pre><code>var title = &quot;this is simple string with finnish word tämä on ääkköstesti älkää ihmetelkö&quot;;
// Does not work
var searchterm = &quot;äl&quot;;

// does not work
//var searchterm = &quot;ää&quot;;

// Works
//var searchterm = &quot;wi&quot;;

if ( new RegExp(&quot;(?:^|$|\\s)&quot;+searchterm, &quot;gi&quot;).test(title) ) {
    $(&quot;#result&quot;).html(&quot;Match: (&quot;+searchterm+&quot;): &quot;+title);
} else {
    $(&quot;#result&quot;).html(&quot;nothing found with term: &quot;+searchterm);   
}
</code></pre>
<p>Breakdown:</p>
<p><code>(?:</code> parenthesis <code>()</code> form a capture group in Regex. Parenthesis started with a question mark and colon <code>?:</code> form a non-capturing group. They just group the terms together</p>
<p><code>^</code> the caret symbol matches the beginning of a string</p>
<p><code>|</code> the bar is the &quot;or&quot; operator.</p>
<p><code>$</code> the dollar symbol matches the end of a string</p>
<p><code>\s</code> matches whitespace (appears as <code>\\s</code> in the string because we have to escape the backslash)</p>
<p><code>)</code> closes the group</p>
<p>So instead of using <code>\b</code>, which matches word boundaries and doesn't work for unicode characters, we use a non-capturing group which matches the beginning of a string OR whitespace.</p>

Q: SwiftUI ToolbarItem borderless button has untappable padding areas A: <p>The bloated shape is the default styling which buttons inside a toolbar get with iOS 26.</p>
<p>Your observation that the interactive glass effect is triggered when tapping the edges of the shape, but the button action is not triggered, is interesting. You could consider reporting this as a bug.</p>
<p>The bloated shape is actually the <em>shared background</em> behind the toolbar item. This is like a container for the items, providing a glass effect. It is also interesting that the shared background on the toolbar item takes on the accent color when a prominent button style is applied to the button being wrapped by the item.</p>
<p>I couldn't find a way to trigger the button action when the edge of the shape is tapped. So I think your choice of workaround boils down to hiding the shared background, or emulating the appearance of the button when it has a shared background.</p>
<p>Here is how you could implement these two workarounds.</p>
<p><strong>Workaround 1: Hide the shared background</strong></p>
<p>The shared background can be hidden by applying <code>.sharedBackgroundVisibility(.hidden)</code> to the <code>ToolbarItem</code>. However, if you try this with your example, the whole button disappears!</p>
<ul>
<li>To keep the button visible, you can apply the glass effect yourself. This needs to be tinted with the accent color.</li>
<li>In order for the text of the label to be shown white when light mode is in effect, the foreground style needs to be set explicitly. Alternatively, a prominent button style can be applied (but a tint still needs to be applied to the glass).</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>ToolbarItem(placement: .bottomBar) {
    Button(&quot;Save&quot;) {
        print(&quot;Tapped!&quot;)
    }
    .glassEffect(.regular.tint(.accentColor).interactive())
    .foregroundStyle(.white)
.padding()
    // .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent) // alternative to .foregroundStyle
    // .buttonStyle(.glassProminent) // another alternative
    // .border(Color.red)         // RED BORDER VISIBLE
}
.sharedBackgroundVisibility(.hidden)
</code></pre>
<p>Using this approach, the button is <em>smaller</em> than the native button with shared background. The glass effect at the edges of the button is also missing, probably because it is stripped away when the shared background is hidden.</p>
<p>So a better workaround might be:</p>
<p><strong>Workaround 2: Emulate the styling of a toolbar button</strong></p>
<p>It is not too difficult to emulate the styling. The modifiers can be encapsulated as a <code>ButtonStyle</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct MyToolbarButtonStyle: ButtonStyle {
    func makeBody(configuration: Configuration) -&gt; some View {
        configuration.label
            .padding(.horizontal, 16)
            .padding(.vertical, 14)
            .frame(minWidth: 44, minHeight: 44)
            .contentShape(.capsule)
            .foregroundStyle(.white)
            .glassEffect(.regular.tint(.accentColor).interactive(), in: .capsule)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>You might like to add a <code>ButtonStyle</code> extension, for convenience:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>extension ButtonStyle where Self == MyToolbarButtonStyle {
    static var myToolbarButton: Self { Self() }
}
</code></pre>
<p>As with workaround 1, if this button style is used on the button in the toolbar (with shared background hidden), the glass effect doesn't work like it does for the native toolbar button (with shared background visible). In particular, the edges don't have the glass border effect. It is also necessary to apply <code>.fixedSize()</code> when the style is used for a toolbar button, otherwise the label may be truncated.</p>
<p>So, an alternative way to show the button is to replace <code>.toolbar</code> with <code>.safeAreaBar(edge: .bottom)</code>. This then gives the same scroll edge effect as you get with a toolbar:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>ContentView()
    .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, maxHeight: .infinity)
    .safeAreaBar(edge: .bottom) {
        Button(&quot;Save&quot;) {
            print(&quot;Tapped!&quot;)
        }
        .buttonStyle(.myToolbarButton)
        // .border(Color.red)         // RED BORDER VISIBLE
    }
</code></pre>
